{
  "id": "grammar.a2.01.04.viaje",
  "title": "Talking About Your Trip",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "The pretérito perfecto becomes useful when you connect recent completed actions with travel and accommodation. Keep the core structure stable: haber + participle. Add travel nouns and verbs around it to say where you have stayed, what you have booked, and what you have done."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "He llegado al hotel.",
          "english": "I have arrived at the hotel."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Hemos reservado una habitación.",
          "english": "We have booked a room."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Han viajado en tren.",
          "english": "They have travelled by train."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "¿Has llegado bien?",
          "english": "Have you arrived safely?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        [
          "he llegado",
          "I have arrived"
        ],
        [
          "hemos reservado",
          "we have booked"
        ],
        [
          "han viajado",
          "they have travelled"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "The participle stays unchanged even when the noun is feminine: He *reservado* una *habitación*."
    },
